  "codebook": {
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator explicitly attributes a positive outcome, action, or achievement to their own efforts, leadership, or advocacy, thereby seeking recognition or responsibility for the result.",
    "clarification": "Credit-claiming is identified when a senator uses language such as 'I secured,' 'I helped,' 'I authored,' 'I announced,' or similar phrasing to directly link themselves to a specific, tangible outcome (e.g., funding, legislation, or policy change). It may also include instances where the senator highlights their role in a process that led to a positive result, even if the outcome is not yet fully realized. The focus is on the senator taking personal ownership of the result.",
    "positive_examples": [
